#4471 was closed as one bug, "dates parse mm/dd in eu locales", with one fix queued. Sorted by
cause, the reports merged into it are two bugs. They break dates at different points on the way from
the booking form to the calendar, they need different fixes, and only one of them had a ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The desk merges on the subject line (<a href="/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject">decision 0001</a>). Both
bugs arrive under the same subjects, so the merge could not see the split, and the smaller bug never
got a ticket of its own.
</Callout>

## The two problems

<TicketList ticket={4471} repo="ricevind/support-kb-4471" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2 }} />

**`parser`** — the shared date parser reads every date month first. A tenant that writes the day
first gets its dates flipped: 7 April comes out as 4 July. This is what #4471 describes, and it is
the bug with the queued fix.

**`hint`** — booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. A tenant that writes the day first
follows the hint and types the month first, and the form then reads that input the tenant's way.
Nothing is wrong with the shared parser here: the hint asks for one order and the form reads the
other. The tenants on booking-v3 produce these reports, and only through the form. Same subject
lines as the bug above, different bug, different fix.

## How the reports were counted

The export repeats some rows: the ticket system's pages overlap, so a report can arrive twice. One
id is one report, so the count starts from unique ids, not from rows.

The cause of a report comes from its tenant, not from what intake tagged it. A tenant has a locale
and a booking form (`src/lib/data/tenants/`), and `causeOf` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts` combines those
with the channel the report came in on. Intake's `meta.intakeTag` is a guess taken from the
reporter's browser, and it disagrees with the cause for a large share of these reports; the counts
here ignore it.

Reports the desk read as a one-day move stay counted where they were filed, and are footnoted under
the table below.

## What was merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A merge on subject lines cannot split a ticket by cause. The cause lives on the tenant and the
  channel, so check it before a ticket is closed as one fix.
- Two bugs that look alike to the reporter can need two fixes. Count them separately before
  engineering plans the work.

